Overfull \hbox (2311.49228pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 6779--6779
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 ble spanning trees. However, there can be more than one minim
um spanning tree of a given graph. Indeed, all spanning trees of an unweighted 
(or equally weighted) graph $G$ are minimum spanning trees, since each contains
 exactly $n-1$ equal-weight edges. Such a spanning tree can be found using eith
er DFS or BFS. Finding a minimum spanning tree is more difficult for general we
ighted graphs. But two different algorithms are presented below, both demonstra
ting the optimality of specific greedy heuristics.[] 
 []


