Overfull \hbox (1040.99597pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 7065--7065
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Dijkstra\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s algorithm is the 
method of choice for finding shortest paths in an edge- and/or vertex-weighted 
graph. Starting from a particular vertex $s$, it finds the shortest path from $
s$ to all other vertices in the graph, including your desired destination $t$.[
] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1733.99396pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 7065--7065
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Suppose the shortest path from $s$ to $t$ in graph $G$ passes
 through a particular intermediate vertex $x$. Clearly, the best $s$-to- $t$ pa
th must contain the shortest path from $s$ to $x$ as its prefix, because if it 
doesn\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 t we can improve the path by starting wi
th the shorter $s$-to- $x$ prefix. Thus, we must compute the shortest path from
 $s$ to $x$ before we find the path from $s$ to $t$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1949.24333pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 7065--7065
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Dijkstra\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s algorithm proceed
s in a series of rounds, where each round establishes the shortest path from $s
$ to some new vertex. Specifically, $x$ is the vertex that minimizes $\operator
name{dist}\left(s, v_{i}\right)+w\left(v_{i}, x\right)$ over all unfinished ver
tices $v_{i}$. Here $w(a, b)$ denotes the weight of the edge from vertex $a$ to
 vertex $b$, and $\operatorname{dist}(a, b)$ is the length of the shortest path
 between them.[] 
 []



[256]
Overfull \hbox (1791.74379pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 7065--7065
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 This suggests a dynamic programming-like strategy. The shorte
st path from $s$ to itself is trivial, so $\operatorname{dist}(s, s)=0 \int^{1}
$ If $(s, y)$ is the lightest edge incident to $s$, then $\operatorname{dist}(s
, y)=w(s, y)$. Once we determine the shortest path to a node $x$, we check all 
the outgoing edges of $x$ to see whether there is a shorter path from $s$ throu
gh $x$ to some unknown vertex.[] 
