Overfull \hbox (2579.2415pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 8528--8528
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Searching for patterns in text strings is a problem of unques
tionable importance. Back in Section 6.7 (page 188 ) I presented algorithms for
 exact string matchingfinding where the pattern string $P$ occurs as a substrin
g of the text string $T$. But life is often not that simple. Words in either th
e text or pattern can be mispelled (sic), robbing us of exact similarity. Evolu
tionary changes in genomic sequences or language usage mean that we often searc
h with archaic patterns in mind: "Thou shalt not kill" morphs over time into "Y
ou should not murder."[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1597.49435pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 8528--8528
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 How can we search for the substring closest to a given patter
n, to compensate for spelling errors? To deal with inexact string matching, we 
must first define a cost function telling us how far apart two strings are. A r
easonable distance measure reflects the number of changes that must be made to 
convert one string to another. There are three natural types of changes:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (269.24821pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 8528--8528
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Substitution - Replace a single character in pattern $P$ wi
th a different character, such as changing shot to spot.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (248.24828pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 8528--8528
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Insertion - Insert a single character into pattern $P$ to h
elp it match text $T$, such as changing ago to agog.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (242.99829pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 8528--8528
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Deletion - Delete a single character from pattern $P$ to he
lp it match text $T$, such as changing hour to our.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1224.74544pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 8528--8528
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Properly posing the question of string similarity requires us
 to set the cost of each such transform operation. Assigning each operation an 
equal cost of 1 defines the edit distance between two strings. Approximate stri
ng matching arises in many applications, as detailed in Section 21.4 (page 688)
.[] 
 []


