shes his king\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s pawn up two squares to start t
he game, and announces "checkmate." The only obvious way to verify that he is r
ight would be to construct the full tree of all your possible moves with his ir
refutable replies and demonstrate that you, in fact, cannot win from the curren
t position. This full tree will have a number of nodes exponential in its heigh
t, which is the number of moves before you lose playing your most spirited poss
ible defense. Clearly this tree cannot be constructed and analyzed in polynomia
l time, so the problem is not in NP.[] 
