rt{2})^{n}$, this runs in $O\left(1.414^{n}\right)$ vs. the naive bound, which 
is a big improvement. For $n=100$, this cuts the number of steps from $1.27 \ti
mes 10^{30}$ to $1.13 \times 10^{15}$. But $(\sqrt{2})^{n}$ still grows exponen
tially, so this is not a polynomial-time algorithm. This quantum algorithm is a
