[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Special efforts must be taken to represent very large graphs 
efficiently. However, interesting problems have been solved on graphs with mill
ions of edges and vertices. The first step is to make your data structure as le
an as possible, by packing your adjacency matrix in a bit vector (see Section 1
5.5 (page 456) or removing unnecessary pointers from your adjacency list repres
entation. For example, in static graphs (which do not support edge insertions o
r deletions) each edge list can be replaced by a packed array of vertex identif
iers, eliminating pointers and thus potentially saving half the space.[] 
