Overfull \hbox (3314.23936pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: The dual problems of integer factorization and pr
imality testing have surprisingly many applications for a problem long suspecte
d of being only of mathematical interest. The security of the RSA public-key cr
yptography system (see Section 21.6 (page 697) is based on the computational in
tractability of factoring large integers. It is known that hash table performan
ce typically improves when the table size is a prime number. To get this benefi
t, an initialization routine must identify a prime near the desired table size.
 Finally, prime numbers are just interesting to play with. This is why the prog
ram to generate large primes used to reside in the games directory of UNIX syst
ems.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1949.24333pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Factoring and primality testing are closely related problems,
 although they are quite different algorithmically. There exist algorithms that
 can demonstrate that an integer is composite (i.e. not prime) without actually
 giving the factors. To convince yourself of the plausibility of this, note tha
t you can demonstrate the compositeness of any non-trivial integer whose last d
igit is $0,2,4,5,6$, or 8 without doing the actual division.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1749.74391pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The simplest algorithm for both of these problems is brute-fo
rce trial division. To factor $n$, compute the remainder of $n / i$ for all $1<
i \leq \sqrt{n}$. The prime factorization of $n$ will contain at least one inst
ance of every $i$ such that $n / i=\lfloor n / i\rfloor$, unless $n$ is prime. 
Make sure you handle the multiplicities correctly, and account for any primes l
arger than $\sqrt{n}$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (920.24632pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Such algorithms can be sped up by using a precomputed table o
f small primes to avoid testing all possible $i$. Surprisingly many primes can 
be represented in surprisingly little space by using bit vectors (see Section 1
5.5 (page 456 ). A bit[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (547.4974pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 vector of all odd numbers less than 1,000,000 fits in under 6
4 kilobytes. Even tighter encodings become possible by eliminating all multiple
s of 3 and other small primes.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3014.99023pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Although trial division runs in $O(\sqrt{n})$ time, it is not
 a polynomial-time algorithm. It only takes $\lg n$ bits to represent $n$, so t
rial division takes time exponential in the input size. Considerably faster (bu
t still exponential time) factoring algorithms exist, whose correctness depends
 upon more substantial number theory. The fastest known algorithm, the number f
ield sieve, uses randomness to construct a system of congruences - the solution
 of which usually gives a factor of the integer. Integers with 250 digits ( 829
 bits) have been factored using this method, although such feats require enormo
us amounts of computation.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2500.49173pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Randomized algorithms make it much easier to test whether an 
integer is prime. Fermat\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s little theorem stat
es that $a^{n-1}=1(\bmod n)$ for all $a$ not divisible by $n$, provided $n$ is 
prime. Suppose we pick a random value $1 \leq a<n$ and compute the residue of $
a^{n-1}(\bmod n)$. If this residue is not 1 , we have just proven that $n$ must
 be composite. Such randomized primality tests are very efficient. PGP (see Sec
tion 21.6 (page 697) finds $300+$ digit primes using hundreds of these tests in
 minutes, for use as cryptographic keys.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2531.99164pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Although the primes are scattered in a seemingly random way t
hroughout the integers, there is some regularity to their distribution. The pri
me number theorem states that the number of primes less than $n$ (commonly deno
ted by $\pi(n))$ is approximately $n / \ln n$. Further, there never are large g
aps between primes, so in general one should expect to examine about $\ln n$ in
tegers to find the first prime larger than $n$. This distribution, coupled with
 the fast randomized primality test, explains how PGP can find such large prime
s so quickly.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2993.9903pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 12063--12063
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Quantum computers are (theoretically!) capable of factoring l
arge integers very fast, indeed exponentially faster than conventional machines
. I would not be shocked if quantum computers capable of fast factoring exist b
efore I write the fourth edition of this book. But serious technical challenges
 remain: a recent proposal to factor RSA-scale (2,048 bit) integers using Shor\
TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s algorithm require 20 million qubits because 
of the need for extensive error correction [GE19]. I\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/
m/n/10 ve heard RSA factoring described as the "suicide app" for quantum comput
ing: because the moment it succeeds, RSA stops being used and the application g
oes away.[] 
