Overfull \hbox (2720.99109pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Set partitions can be generated using techniques akin to inte
ger partitions. Each set partition is encoded as a restricted growth function, 
$a_{1}, \ldots, a_{n}$, where $a_{1}=0$ and $a_{i} \leq 1+\max \left(a_{1}, \ld
ots, a_{i-1}\right)$, for $i=2, \ldots, n$. Each distinct digit identifies a su
bset, or block, of the partition, while the growth condition ensures that the b
locks are sorted into a canonical order based on the smallest element in each b
lock. For example, the restricted growth function $0,1,1,2,0,3,1$ defines the s
et partition $\{\{1,5\},\{2,3,7\},\{4\},\{6\}\}$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1513.4946pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Since there is a one-to-one equivalence between set partition
s and restricted growth functions, we can use lexicographic order on the restri
cted growth functions to order the partitions. Indeed, the fifteen set partitio
ns of $\{1,2,3,4\}$ listed above are sequenced according to the lexicographic o
rder of their restricted growth function (check it out).[] 
