Overfull \hbox (2353.49216pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: Graph generation typically arises when constructi
ng test data for programs. Perhaps you have two different programs that solve t
he same problem, and you want to see which is faster, or make sure that they bo
th give the same (presumably right) answer. Another application is experimental
 graph theory, verifying whether a particular property is true for all graphs. 
It is much easier to believe the four-color theorem after you have demonstrated
 four colorings for all planar graphs on fifteen vertices.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1067.2459pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Many factors complicate the problem of generating graphs. Fir
st, make sure you know exactly what type of graph you want to generate. Figure 
7.2 on page 198 illustrates several important properties of graphs. For purpose
s of generation, the most important questions are:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3770.98804pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Do I want labeled or unlabeled graphs? - The issue here is 
whether the names of the vertices matter in deciding whether two graphs are the
 same. In generating labeled graphs, we seek to construct all possible labeling
s of all possible graph topologies. When generating unlabeled graphs, we want e
xactly one representative for each topology and ignore labelings. For example, 
there are only two connected unlabeled graphs on three vertices - a triangle an
d a simple path. However, there are four connected labeled graphs on three vert
ices - one triangle and three 3 -vertex paths, each distinguished by the name o
f their central vertex. In general, labeled graphs are much easier to generate.
 However, you are likely to get swamped with many isomorphic copies of the same
 few graphs.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2248.49246pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Do I want directed or undirected graphs? - Most natural gen
eration algorithms generate undirected graphs. These can be turned into directe
d graphs by flipping coins to orient the edges. Any graph can be oriented to be
 directed and acyclic (i.e., a DAG) by randomly permuting the vertices on a lin
e and aiming each edge from left to right. With all such ideas, careful thought
 must be given to decide whether you are generating all graphs uniformly at ran
dom, and how much this matters to you.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (563.24736pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 You also must define what you mean by random. There are three
 primary models of random graphs, all of which generate graphs according to dif
ferent probability distributions:[] 
 []

Missing character: There is no ő ("151) in font ec-lmtt10!

Overfull \hbox (1576.49442pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Random edge generation - The Erds-Rényi model is parameteri
zed by a given edge probability $p$. In this model, a coin is flipped for each 
pair of vertices $x$ and $y$ to decide whether to add an edge $(x, y)$. All lab
eled graphs will be generated with equal probability when $p=1 / 2$, although s
maller values of $p$ can be used to construct sparser random graphs.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1870.49356pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Random edge selection - The second model is parameterized b
y the desired number of edges $m$. It selects $m$ distinct edges uniformly at r
andom. One way to do this is by drawing random $(x, y)$-pairs and creating an e
dge if that pair is not already in the graph. An alternative approach construct
s the set of $\binom{n}{2}$ possible edges and selects a random $m$-subset of t
hem, as discussed in Section 17.5 (page 521).[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2243.24248pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Preferential attachment - Under a rich-get-richer model, ne
wly created edges are more likely to point to high-degree vertices than low-deg
ree ones. Consider new links (edges) being added to the graph of webpages. Unde
r any realistic web generation model, it is much more likely the next link will
 point to Google than www.algorist.com ${ }^{1}$ Selecting the next neighbor wi
th probability proportional to its degree yields graphs with power law properti
es encountered in many real networks.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1571.24443pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Which of these options best models your application? Probably
 none of them. By definition, random graphs have very little concrete structure
. But graphs are used to model relationships, which are often highly structured
. Experiments conducted on random graphs, although interesting and easy to perf
orm, often fail to capture the phenomenon that you are looking for.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2474.2418pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 An alternative to random graphs is "organic" graphs-graphs th
at reflect the relationships among real-world objects. Many raw sources of rela
tionships are available on the web that can be turned into interesting organic 
graphs with a little programming and imagination. Consider the graph defined by
 a set of webpages, with any hyperlink between two pages defining an edge. Or, 
what about the graph implicit in railroad, subway, or airline networks, with ve
rtices being stations and edges between two stations connected by direct servic
e?[] 
 []


