[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Problem description: Identify the different pieces or compone
nts of $G$, where vertices $x$ and $y$ are in different components when no path
 exists from $x$ to $y$ in $G$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (736.49686pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: The connected components of a graph represent in 
a gross sense the separate pieces of the graph. Two vertices are in the same co
mponent of $G$ if and only if there exists some path between them.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1513.4946pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Finding connected components is at the heart of many importan
t graph applications. For example, consider the challenge of identifying natura
l clusters among a set of items. We represent each item by a vertex, and add an
 edge between each pair of items deemed "similar." The connected components of 
this graph correspond to the different classes of items.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1597.49435pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Testing whether a graph is connected is an essential preproce
ssing step for every graph algorithm. Subtle, hard-to-detect bugs often result 
when an algorithm is run only on one component of a disconnected graph. Connect
ivity tests are so quick and easy that you should always verify the integrity o
f your input graph, even when you know that it just has to be connected.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3051.74013pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Testing the connectivity of any undirected graph is a job for
 either depthfirst or breadth-first search. Which one you choose doesn\TS1/lmtt
/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 t really matter. Both traversals initialize a componen
t-number field for each vertex to 0 , and then start the search for component 1
 from vertex $v_{1}$. As each vertex is discovered, the value of this field is 
set to the current component number. After the initial traversal ends, the comp
onent number is incremented, and the search begins again from the first vertex 
whose component-number remains 0 . Properly implemented using adjacency lists (
as is done in Section 7.7.1 (page 218) this runs in $O(n+m)$ time.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3440.239pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - What if my graph is directed? - There are two distinct noti
ons of connected components for directed graphs. A directed graph is strongly c
onnected if there is a directed path between every pair of vertices. A directed
 graph is weakly connected if it would be connected when ignoring the direction
 of edges. This distinction can be made clear by considering the network of one
- and two-way streets in a given city. The network is strongly connected if it 
is possible to drive legally between any two places in town. The network is wea
kly connected when it is possible to legally or illegally drive between any pai
r of positions. The network is disconnected if there is no possible way to driv
e from some $a$ to some $b$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (941.24626pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Weakly and strongly connected components define unique partit
ions of the vertices. The output figure above shows a directed graph consisting
 of two weakly connected components, and five strongly connected components (al
so called blocks of $G$ ).[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3319.48935pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Testing whether a directed graph is weakly connected can be d
one easily in linear time. Simply turn all edges of $G$ into undirected edges a
nd use the DFS-based connected components algorithm described previously. Tests
 for strong connectivity are somewhat more complicated. The simplest linear-tim
e algorithm performs a depth-first search from any vertex $v$ to demonstrate th
at the entire graph is reachable from $v$. We then construct a transpose graph 
$G^{\prime}$ where we reverse all the edges of $G$. A traversal of $G^{\prime}$
 from $v$ suffices to determine whether all vertices of $G$ can reach $v$. Grap
h $G$ is strongly connected iff all vertices can reach, and are reachable, from
 $v$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1019.99603pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 All the strongly connected components of $G$ can be extracted
 in linear time using more sophisticated DFS-based algorithms. A generalization
 of the above "two-DFS" approach is deceptively easy to program, but somewhat s
ubtle to understand exactly why it works:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (326.99805pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 1. Perform a DFS, starting from an arbitrary vertex in $G$, a
nd labeling each vertex in order of its completion (not discovery).[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (11.99896pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 2. Reverse the direction of each edge in $G$, yielding $G^{\p
rime}$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (783.74672pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 3. Perform a DFS of $G^{\prime}$, starting from the highest n
umbered vertex in $G$. If this search does not completely traverse $G^{\prime}$
, perform a new search starting from the highest-numbered unvisited vertex.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (43.49887pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 4. Each DFS tree created in Step 3 defines a strongly connect
ed component.[] 
