Overfull \hbox (1109.24577pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: Topological sort arises as a subproblem in most a
lgorithms on directed acyclic graphs. Topological sorting orders the vertices a
nd edges of a DAG in a simple and consistent way, and hence plays the same role
 for DAGs that depth-first search does for general graphs.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1754.9939pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Topological sort can be used to schedule jobs under precedenc
e constraints. Suppose we have a set of tasks to do, but certain tasks must be 
performed before other ones. These precedence constraints form a directed acycl
ic graph, and any topological sort (also known as a linear extension) defines a
n order to perform these tasks such that each is done only after all of its con
straints are satisfied.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (342.748pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 1. Only DAGs can be topologically sorted, because any directe
d cycle provides an inherent contradiction to a linear order of tasks.[] 
