[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Prim\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s algorithm - Starts 
from an arbitrary vertex $v$ to "grow" a tree, repeatedly finding the lowest-co
st edge that links some new vertex into this tree. During execution, we label e
ach vertex as either in the tree, in the fringe (meaning there exists an edge f
rom a tree vertex), or unseen (meaning the vertex is still more than one edge a
way from the tree).[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (405.74782pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 select minimum-weight edge between tree and fringe add the se
lected edge and vertex to the tree update the cost to all affected fringe verti
ces[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1282.49527pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 This creates a spanning tree for any connected graph, since n
o cycle can be introduced by adding edges between tree and fringe vertices. Tha
t this results in a tree of minimum weight can be proven by contradiction. Prim
\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s algorithm can be implemented in $O\left(n^{
2}\right)$ time using simple data structures.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2222.24254pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Boruvka\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s algorithm - This
 rests on the observation that the lowest-weight edge incident on every vertex 
must appear in the minimum spanning tree. The union of these edges will result 
in a spanning forest with at most $n / 2$ trees. Now select an edge $(x, y)$ of
 lowest weight for each of tree $T$, such that $x \in T$ and $y \notin T$. All 
of these edges must again appear in a minimum spanning tree, and so again resul
ts in a spanning forest with at most half as many trees as before:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (284.99817pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 for each $T$ in $F$, find the smallest edge from $T$ to $G-T$
 add all selected edges to $F$, thus merging pairs of trees[] 
 []



[379]
Overfull \hbox (799.49667pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The number of trees are at least halved in each round, so we 
get the MST after at most $\lg n$ iterations, each of which takes linear time. 
This gives an $O(m \log n)$ algorithm without using any fancy data structures.[
] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (384.74788pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 MST is only one of several spanning tree problems that arise 
in practice. The following questions will help you sort your way through them:[
] 
