[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Problem description: For transitive closure, construct a grap
h $G^{\prime}$ with edge $(i, j) \in E^{\prime}$ iff there is a directed path f
rom $i$ to $j$ in $G$. For transitive reduction, construct a graph $G^{\prime}$
 with the smallest number of edges such that a directed path from $i$ to $j$ ex
ists in $G^{\prime}$ iff there is a directed path from $i$ to $j$ in $G$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1172.24559pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: Transitive closure can be thought of as establish
ing a data structure to efficiently solve reachability queries: "Can I get to $
y$ from $x$ ?" After constructing a transitive closure matrix $M$, such queries
 can be answered in constant time by reporting matrix entry $M[x, y]$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2358.74214pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Transitive closure arises in propagating the consequences of 
modified attributes of a graph $G$. Consider the graph underlying a spreadsheet
 model, whose vertices are cells with an edge from cell $i$ to cell $j$ iff the
 result of cell $j$ depends on cell $i$. When a given cell is modified, the val
ues of all cells reachable from it must also be updated. The identity of these 
cells is revealed by the transitive closure of $G$. Many database problems redu
ce to computing transitive closures, for analogous reasons.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1.499pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 There are three basic algorithms for computing transitive clo
sure:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1576.49442pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - The simple algorithm performs a breadth-first or depth-firs
t search from each vertex, and keeps track of all vertices encountered. Doing $
n$ such traversals gives an $O(n(n+m))$ algorithm, which degenerates to cubic t
ime when the graph is dense. This algorithm is easily implemented, runs well on
 sparse graphs, and is likely the right answer for your application.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (904.49637pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Warshall\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s algorithm const
ructs the transitive closure in $O\left(n^{3}\right)$ time, using a slick appro
ach identical to Floyd\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s all-pairs shortest-pa
th algorithm of Section 18.4 (page 554. If we are not interested in the length 
of the[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (668.24706pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 resulting paths, we can reduce storage by retaining only one 
bit per matrix element. Thus, $D_{i j}^{k}=$ true iff $j$ is reachable from $i$
 using only vertices $1, \ldots, k$ as intermediates.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2474.2418pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Matrix multiplication can also be used to solve transitive 
closure. Let $M^{1}$ be the adjacency matrix of graph $G$. The non-zero matrix 
entries of $M^{2}=M \times M$ identify all length-2 paths in $G$. Observe that 
$M^{2}[i, j]=$ $\sum_{x} M[i, x] \cdot M[x, j]$, so path $(i, x, j)$ contribute
s to $M^{2}[i, j]$. Thus, the union $\cup_{i}^{n} M^{i}$ yields the transitive 
closure $T$. Furthermore, this union can be computed using only $O(\lg n)$ matr
ix operations using the fast exponentiation algorithm in Section 16.9 (page 493
 .[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1061.99591pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 This will be faster for large enough $n$ if using Strassen\TS
1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s fast matrix multiplication algorithm, although
 I for one wouldn\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 t bother trying. Transitive 
closure is provably as hard as matrix multiplication, so there is little hope f
or a significantly faster algorithm.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2988.74031pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The running time for all three of these methods can be substa
ntially improved on many graphs. Recall that a strongly connected component is 
a set of vertices for which all pairs are mutually reachable. For example, any 
directed cycle defines a strongly connected subgraph. All the vertices in any s
trongly connected component must reach exactly the same subset of $G$. Thus, we
 can reduce our problem to finding the transitive closure on a graph of strongl
yconnected components, that generally has fewer edges and vertices than $G$. Th
e strongly connected components of $G$ can be computed in linear time (see Sect
ion 18.1 (page 542 ).[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2542.49161pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Transitive reduction (also known as minimum equivalent digrap
h) is the inverse operation to transitive closure, namely reducing the number o
f edges while maintaining identical reachability properties. The transitive clo
sure of $G$ is identical to the transitive closure of the transitive reduction 
of $G$. Transitive reduction is space minimization, by eliminating redundant ed
ges from $G$ that do not affect reachability. It also arises in graph drawing, 
where it is important to eliminate as many implied edges as possible to reduce 
visual clutter.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (836.24657pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Although the transitive closure of $G$ is uniquely defined, a
 graph may have many different transitive reductions, including $G$ itself. We 
seek the smallest such reduction, but there are multiple formulations of the pr
oblem:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1408.4949pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - A quick-and-dirty, linear-time transitive reduction algorit
hm identifies the strongly connected components of $G$, replaces each by a simp
le directed cycle, and then adds the edges bridging the different components. A
lthough this does not always yield the smallest possible reduction, it is likel
y to be pretty close on many graphs.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (584.2473pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 One catch with this heuristic is that it might add edges to t
he transitive reduction of $G$ that are not in $G$. Depending on your applicati
on, this may or may not be a problem.[] 
