Overfull \hbox (2510.9917pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 A de Bruijn sequence $S$ of span $k$ on an alphabet $\Sigma$ 
of size $\alpha$ is a circular string of length $\alpha^{k}$ containing all str
ings of length $k$ as substrings of $S$, each exactly once. For example, for $k
=3$ and $\Sigma=\{0,1\}$, the circular string 00011101 contains the following s
ubstrings in order: $000,001,011,111,110,101,010,100$. De Bruijn sequences can 
be thought of as "safe cracker" sequences, describing the shortest sequence of 
dial turns with $\alpha$ positions sufficient to try out all combinations of le
ngth $n$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1613.24431pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 De Bruijn sequences can be constructed by building a directed
 graph whose vertices represent all $\alpha^{n-1}$ strings of length $n-1$, wit
h an edge $(u, v)$ iff $u=s_{1} s_{2} \ldots s_{n-1}$ and $v=s_{2} \ldots s_{n-
1} s_{n}$. Any Eulerian cycle on this graph describes a de Bruijn sequence. Exp
ositions on de Bruijn sequences and their construction include Eve11 PS03].[] 
 []


