[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 A cynical view of graph algorithms is that "everything we wan
t to do is hard." Indeed, all problems in this section are provably NP-complete
 with the exception of graph isomorphism - whose complexity status remains an o
pen question. The theory of NP-completeness demonstrates that either all NP-com
plete problems have polynomial-time algorithms, or none of them do. The former 
prospect is sufficiently unlikely that NP-completeness suffices to say that no 
efficient algorithm exists to solve the given problem.[] 
