Overfull \hbox (2479.49179pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: The need to find large independent sets arises in
 facility dispersion problems, where we seek a set of mutually separated locati
ons. To ensure that no two locations of our new "McAlgorithm" franchise service
 are so close as to compete with each other, we can create a graph where the ve
rtices represent possible locations, and add edges between any two locations de
emed close enough to interfere. The maximum independent set on this graph defin
es the largest set of franchise locations we can sell without cannibalizing sal
es.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1959.7433pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Independent sets (also known as stable sets) avoid conflicts 
between elements, and hence often arise in coding theory and scheduling problem
s. For instance, we can define a graph whose vertices represent the set of poss
ible code words, and add edges between any two code words sufficiently similar 
to be confused due to noise. The maximum independent set of this graph defines 
the highest capacity code for the given communication channel.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (17.24895pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Independent set is closely related to two other NP-complete p
roblems:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2778.74092pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Clique - A clique is a subset of pairwise connected vertice
s, while an independent set is a subset of pairwise non-connected vertices. The
 complement of $G=(V, E)$ is a graph $G^{\prime}=\left(V, E^{\prime}\right)$ wh
ere $(i, j) \in E^{\prime}$ iff $(i, j)$ is not in $E$. The complement replaces
 each edge by a non-edge and vice versa. The maximum independent set in $G$ is 
exactly the maximum clique in $G^{\prime}$, so the two problems are algorithmic
ally identical. The algorithms and implementations in Section 19.1 (page 586) c
an thus be used to find the independent set of $G^{\prime}$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1172.24559pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Vertex coloring - The vertex coloring of a graph $G=(V, E)$
 is a partition of $V$ into a $k$ sets (colors), where no two vertices of the s
ame color can have an edge between them. Each color class defines an independen
t set. Many independent set applications are really coloring problems.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1198.49551pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Indeed, one heuristic to find a large independent set is to u
se any vertex coloring algorithm/heuristic, and take the largest color class. O
ne consequence of this observation is that all graphs with small chromatic numb
ers (such as planar and bipartite graphs) must have large independent sets.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1781.24382pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The simplest reasonable heuristic is to find the lowest-degre
e vertex, add it to the independent set, and then delete it and all vertices ad
jacent to it. Repeating this process until the graph is empty gives a maximal i
ndependent set, in that it can\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 t be made large
r by just adding vertices. Using randomization or perhaps some degree of exhaus
tive search might result in somewhat larger independent sets.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1539.74452pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The independent set problem is in some sense dual to graph ma
tching. The former asks for a large set of vertices with no edge in common, whi
le the latter asks for a large set of edges with no vertex in common. This sugg
ests trying to rephrase your problem as an efficiently computable matching prob
lem instead of maximum independent set, which is NP-complete.[] 
 []



[391]
Overfull \hbox (993.74611pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The maximum independent set of a tree can be found in linear 
time by (1) stripping off the leaf nodes, (2) adding them to the independent se
t, (3) deleting all adjacent nodes, and then (4) repeating the first step on th
e resulting trees until it is empty.[] 
 []


