Overfull \hbox (2075.24297pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: That motion planning is a complex problem is obvi
ous to anyone who has tried to move a large piece of furniture into a small apa
rtment. Plotting paths for mobile robots is the canonical motion-planning appli
cation. It also arises in systems for molecular docking. Many drugs are small m
olecules that act by binding to a given target model. Identifying which binding
 sites are accessible to a candidate drug is clearly an instance of motion plan
ning.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1964.99329pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Finally, motion planning provides a tool for computer animati
on and virtual reality. Given the set of object models and where they appear in
 scenes $s_{1}$ and $s_{2}$, a motion planning algorithm can construct a short 
sequence of intermediate motions to transform $s_{1}$ to $s_{2}$. These motions
 can fill in the intermediate scenes between $s_{1}$ and $s_{2}$, with such sce
ne interpolation greatly reducing the workload on the animator.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2232.74251pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Is your robot a point? - For point robots, motion planning 
reduces to finding the shortest path from $s$ to $t$ around the obstacles. This
 problem is known as geometric shortest path. The most readily implementable ap
proach constructs the visibility graph of the polygonal obstacles, plus the poi
nts $s$ and $t$. This visibility graph contains a node for each obstacle vertex
, and an edge between two obstacle vertices iff they "see" each other without b
eing blocked by some obstacle edge.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (505.49753pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 This visibility graph can be constructed by testing each of t
he $\binom{n}{2}$ vertexpair edge candidates for intersection against each of t
he $n$ obstacle edges,[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1261.49533pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 although faster algorithms are known. Assign each edge of thi
s visibility graph with weight equal to its length. Then the shortest path from
 $s$ to $t$ can be found using Dijkstra\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s shor
test-path algorithm (see Section 18.4 (page 554 ) in time bounded by what is re
quired to construct the visibility graph.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (909.74635pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - What motions can your robot perform? - Motion planning beco
mes considerably more difficult when the robot becomes a polygon instead of a p
oint. Now all of the corridors that we use must be wide enough to permit the ro
bot to pass through.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2211.74257pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The algorithmic complexity depends upon the number of degrees
 of freedom that the robot can use to move. Is it free to rotate as well as to 
translate? Does the robot have links that are free to bend or to rotate indepen
dently, as in an arm with a hand? Each degree of freedom corresponds to a dimen
sion in the search space of possible configurations. Additional freedom makes i
t more likely that a short path exists from start to goal, although it also mak
es it harder to find this path.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2395.49203pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Can you simplify the shape of your robot? - Motion planning
 algorithms tend to be complex and time-consuming. Anything you can do to simpl
ify your environment is a win. In particular, consider replacing your robot in 
an enclosing disk. Any start-to-goal path for this disk defines such a path for
 the robot inside of it. Furthermore, since any orientation of a disk is equiva
lent to any other orientation, rotation provides no help in finding a path. All
 movements can thus be limited to the simpler case of translation.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3193.48972pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Are motions limited to translation only? - When rotation is
 not allowed, the expanded obstacles approach can be used to reduce the problem
 of polygonal motion planning to the previously resolved case of a point robot.
 Pick a reference point on the robot, and replace each obstacle by its Minkowsk
i sum with the robot polygon (see Section 20.16 (page 674). This creates a larg
er, fatter obstacle, defined by the shadow traced as the robot walks a loop aro
und the object while maintaining contact with it. Finding a path from the initi
al reference position to the goal amidst these fattened obstacles defines a leg
al path for the polygonal robot in the original environment.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2678.99121pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Are the obstacles known in advance? - We have assumed that 
the robot starts out with a map of its environment. But this can\TS1/lmtt/m/n/1
0 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 t be true (say) in applications where the obstacles move. Th
ere are two strategies for solving motion planning problems without a map. The 
first approach explores the environment, building a map of what has been seen, 
and then uses this map to plan a path to the goal. A simpler strategy proceeds 
like a sightless man with a compass. Walk in the direction towards the goal unt
il progress is blocked by an obstacle, and then trace out a path along the[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (431.99774pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 obstacle until the robot is again free to proceed directly to
wards the goal. Unfortunately, this will fail in environments of sufficient com
plexity.[] 
