Overfull \hbox (2983.49033pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Arrangements generalize naturally beyond two dimensions. Inst
ead of lines, the space decomposition is defined by planes (or beyond three dim
ensions, hyperplanes). The zone theorem states that any arrangement of $n d$-di
mensional hyperplanes has total complexity $O\left(n^{d}\right)$, and any singl
e hyperplane intersects cells of complexity $O\left(n^{d-1}\right)$. This provi
des the justification for incremental construction algorithms for arrangements.
 Walking around the boundary of each cell to find the next cell that the hyperp
lane intersects takes time proportional to the number of cells created by inser
ting the hyperplane.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1277.24529pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The history of the zone theorem has become somewhat muddled, 
because the original proofs were later found to be wrong in higher dimensions. 
See ESS93 for a discussion and a correct proof. The theory of Davenport-Schinze
l sequences is intimately tied into the study of arrangements, which is present
ed in SA95.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1985.99323pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 18033--18033
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The naive algorithm for sweeping an arrangement of lines sort
s the $n^{2}$ intersection points by $x$-coordinate and hence requires $O\left(
n^{2} \lg n\right)$ time. The topological sweep EG89, EG91] eliminates the need
 to sort, and so traverses the arrangement in quadratic time. This algorithm is
 readily implementable and can be applied to speed up many sweep-line algorithm
s. See RSS02 for a robust implementation with experimental results.[] 
